---
import Callout from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ContentSection from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/ContentSection.astro";
import PackageInfo from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/PackageInfo.astro";
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<DocsPage
	title="Getting Started"
	description="Install and set up react-socket, a TypeScript-first WebSocket manager for React."
>
	<ContentSection label="What is react-socket?">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			WebSockets look easy until you actually build with them. The initial
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">new WebSocket(url)</code
			> takes five minutes. Then you spend the next week wiring up reconnection
			logic, figuring out why your subscriptions vanish after a dropped connection,
			deduplicating messages when three components all subscribe to the same
			channel, and tracking which sends actually made it to the server. All
			of this ends up scattered across hooks, effects, and refs until the whole
			thing becomes a maintenance problem.
		</p>
		<p class="text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
			react-socket takes that entire layer off your plate. You define your
			message types, wire up a few callbacks, and the library handles the
			socket lifecycle, reconnection, keep-alive, subscription management,
			and in-flight tracking. Your code stays focused on application
			logic. The socket plumbing just works.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Rationale">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			Most WebSocket libraries fall into two camps. The heavy ones give
			you an opinionated protocol, built-in state management, and
			abstractions you end up fighting against the moment your use case
			doesn't fit their mold. The thin ones hand you a WebSocket wrapper
			and leave every hard problem on your plate: reconnection,
			subscription deduplication, delivery tracking, message routing. You
			still write all the same boilerplate.
		</p>
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			react-socket sits in the space between. It's an <strong
				class="text-white/80">orchestrator</strong
			>, not a framework. It manages the transport concerns that are
			genuinely painful to get right, and gets out of the way for
			everything else. Your protocol. Your state management. Your
			serialization format. The library doesn't make decisions it can't
			reverse.
		</p>
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			Here's what you get for free:
		</p>
		<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-white/60 mb-4">
			<li>
				<strong class="text-white/80">Automatic reconnection</strong> with
				exponential backoff and jitter. Subscriptions are restored transparently
				after every reconnect.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong class="text-white/80"
					>Subscription deduplication.</strong
				> Ten components can subscribe to the same channel and only one message
				goes to the server. The library ref-counts internally. When the last
				component unmounts, the unsubscribe fires automatically.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong class="text-white/80"
					>In-flight message tracking.</strong
				> Send a message with an ack ID, track it as pending, and know exactly
				when the server confirmed it or when it was dropped.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong class="text-white/80">Keep-alive pings</strong> so proxies
				and load balancers don't kill idle connections.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong class="text-white/80">First-class TypeScript.</strong> Your
				client and server message types flow through every callback, every
				<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">send()</code>, every
				received message. Protocol mismatches are caught at compile
				time, not in production.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p class="text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
			And here's what you stay in control of: your message format, your
			state management, your routing logic, your UI. react-socket doesn't
			render anything, doesn't touch your store, and doesn't assume your
			protocol. It keeps the socket lifecycle organized and predictable.
			You write the business logic.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Installation">
		<CodeBlock
			code="npm install @luciodale/react-socket"
			language="bash"
			filename="terminal"
		/>
		<PackageInfo
			packageName="@luciodale/react-socket"
			githubRepo="luciodale/react-socket"
		/>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Quick Start">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			The simplest way to use react-socket. Create a manager in a
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">useMemo</code>, store
			the response in
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">useState</code>, fire a
			message, done. Everything lives in a single component:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`import { useMemo, useState } from "react";
import { WebSocketManager } from "@luciodale/react-socket";

type TClientMsg = { action: "echo"; text: string };
type TServerMsg = { action: "echo"; text: string };

function EchoDemo() {
  const [response, setResponse] = useState<string | null>(null);

  const manager = useMemo(() => {
    const m = new WebSocketManager<TClientMsg, TServerMsg>({
      url: "ws://localhost:3001/ws",
      serialize: (msg) => JSON.stringify(msg),
      deserialize: (raw) => JSON.parse(raw),

      onMessageReceived(msg) {
        setResponse(msg.text);
      },
    });
    m.connect();
    return m;
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => manager.send({ data: { action: "echo", text: "hello" } })}>
        Send
      </button>
      {response && <p>Server said: {response}</p>}
    </div>
  );
}`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="EchoDemo.tsx"
		/>
		<p class="text-white/60 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
			That's the whole thing. The manager handles the connection,
			serialization, and message routing. You define what to send and what
			to do when something comes back. Notice how
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">TClientMsg</code> and
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">TServerMsg</code> are fully
			typed: if you change a field name or add a new message variant, TypeScript
			tells you everywhere that needs updating.
		</p>
		<Callout type="tip" title="Lifecycle">
			In a real app, call <code>manager.connect()</code> inside a <code
				>useEffect</code
			>
			and return <code>manager.disconnect()</code> as cleanup. The quick start
			above inlines it in <code>useMemo</code> for brevity.
		</Callout>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Subscriptions: One Message, Many Readers">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			Subscriptions are the core pattern for real-time data. You send a
			message to the server saying "I want updates on this topic," and the
			server pushes data back over time. The powerful part: if five
			components subscribe to the same channel, only one message goes to
			the server. The library ref-counts internally and all components
			read from the same store, updated by a single <code
				class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">onMessageReceived</code
			> callback.
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`import { useEffect } from "react";

function PriceTracker({ ticker }: { ticker: string }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    manager.subscribe(
      \`prices:\${ticker}\`,
      { action: "subscribe", channel: ticker }
    );
    return () => {
      manager.unsubscribe(
        \`prices:\${ticker}\`,
        { action: "unsubscribe", channel: ticker }
      );
    };
  }, [ticker]);

  const price = useStore((s) => s.prices[ticker]);
  return <span>{price}</span>;
}

// Render this 10 times with the same ticker.
// One subscribe message hits the server. All 10 components stay in sync.`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="PriceTracker.tsx"
		/>
		<p class="text-white/60 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
			No duplicate messages, no leaked subscriptions, no manual
			bookkeeping. When the connection drops and comes back, the library
			automatically re-subscribes to every active channel using the stored
			payload. Your components don't even notice.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Fire and Forget vs. Acknowledged Sends">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			react-socket supports two sending patterns, and you pick per
			message:
		</p>
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			<strong class="text-white/80">Fire and forget</strong> is the simple case.
			You send a message and move on. No tracking, no confirmation. Great for
			cursor positions, typing indicators, or anything where a lost message
			doesn't matter because the next one is already on the way.
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`// Fire and forget: send and move on
manager.send({ data: { action: "cursor_move", x: 100, y: 200 } });`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="fire-and-forget.ts"
		/>
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
			<strong class="text-white/80">Acknowledged sends</strong> are for when
			delivery matters. You include an <code
				class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">ackId</code
			> and the library tracks the message as "in-flight" until the server echoes
			it back. The key: the server must include the same ID in its response
			so the library can match the confirmation to the original send.
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`// Acknowledged send: track until the server confirms
const id = crypto.randomUUID();

manager.send({
  data: { action: "message", id, channel: "general", text: "hello" },
  ackId: id,
});

// Later, when the server echoes the message back:
onMessageReceived(msg) {
  if (msg.action === "message") {
    manager.ackInFlight(msg.id); // removes from in-flight tracking
  }
}`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="acknowledged-send.ts"
		/>
		<p class="text-white/60 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
			The <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">onSendIntent</code> callback
			fires on every
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">send()</code>, even when
			offline. This is where you do optimistic UI updates: add the message
			to your store immediately, then confirm or roll back when the server
			responds. If the connection drops while messages are in-flight,
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">onInFlightDrop</code> fires
			so you can mark them as failed or save them for retry.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Callbacks as Dispatchers">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			The idea is simple: you use the manager's callbacks as dispatchers
			and organize your logic in blocks based on the message types you
			receive. Instead of scattering WebSocket handling across components,
			everything routes through one place. Like a reducer, but for your
			socket:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`const manager = new WebSocketManager<TClientMsg, TServerMsg>({
  url: "wss://api.example.com/ws",
  serialize: (msg) => JSON.stringify(msg),
  deserialize: (raw) => JSON.parse(raw),

  onMessageReceived(msg) {
    switch (msg.action) {
      case "echo":
        handleEcho(msg);
        break;
      case "subscribe_ack":
        manager.resolvePendingSubscription(\`chat:\${msg.channel}\`);
        break;
      case "message":
        manager.ackInFlight(msg.id);
        handleChatMessage(msg);
        break;
    }
  },

  onSendIntent({ data, ackId }) {
    if (data.action === "message" && ackId) {
      addOptimisticMessage(data, ackId);
    }
  },
});`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="manager.ts"
		/>
		<p class="text-white/60 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
			The library isn't trying to be magic. It's an orchestrator that
			keeps things organized and functional. You write the logic, the
			manager makes sure it fires at the right time with the right data,
			fully typed.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="TypeScript All the Way Down">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			This is not TypeScript as an afterthought. Your
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">TClientMsg</code> and
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">TServerMsg</code> types are
			the single source of truth for your entire WebSocket protocol. They flow
			through the constructor, every callback, <code
				class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">send()</code
			>,
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">serialize</code>,
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">deserialize</code>, and
			subscription payloads.
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`// These union types define your protocol.
// Change a field here and TypeScript flags every callsite.

type TClientMsg =
  | { action: "echo"; text: string }
  | { action: "subscribe"; channel: string }
  | { action: "message"; id: string; channel: string; text: string };

type TServerMsg =
  | { action: "echo"; text: string }
  | { action: "subscribe_ack"; channel: string }
  | { action: "message"; id: string; channel: string; sender: string; text: string };`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="types.ts"
		/>
		<p class="text-white/60 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
			As your protocol grows, these types keep everything in sync across
			the entire codebase. Add a new message variant, rename a field,
			change a payload shape: the compiler tells you what broke before you
			ever run the code.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Next Steps">
		<ul class="space-y-2 text-white/60">
			<li>
				<a
					href="/docs/configuration"
					class="text-accent hover:underline">Configuration</a
				> — all manager options in detail
			</li>
			<li>
				<a
					href="/docs/subscriptions"
					class="text-accent hover:underline">Subscriptions</a
				> — channels with automatic deduplication
			</li>
			<li>
				<a
					href="/docs/optimistic-updates"
					class="text-accent hover:underline">Sending Messages</a
				> — fire-and-forget and acknowledged sends
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="/docs/api" class="text-accent hover:underline"
					>API Reference</a
				> — every method and type
			</li>
		</ul>
	</ContentSection>
</DocsPage>
